    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAAMX//3/A\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/contrib/ec2/core.py",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 48,
      "author": {
        "id": 4
      },
      "writtenOn": "2012-12-11T14:15:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I really don\u0027t understand why you want to use this pattern? It\u0027s redundant with \u0027from keystone import token\u0027 if you just leave \u0027keystone.token.controllers\u0027 in the \u0027token\u0027 namespace. I don\u0027t understand what it buys you other than more time spent fussing over import management.",
      "revId": "c0967193c679b2dacbc0b94245d43f7882d4e5b2",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAAMX//33U\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/contrib/ec2/core.py",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 48,
      "author": {
        "id": 2218
      },
      "writtenOn": "2012-12-11T15:54:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t particularly like this pattern, but it seems to be a necessary evil.\n\nIf token.controllers is in the namespace, we have a circular dependency.  Core and controllers cannot be in the same namespace, because controllers need to point at multiple core files.  This is one of the few places where a file needs to talk to both a core and a controller file.\n\nWe might be able to change this to:\n\nfrom keystone.token import controllers as token, and then we can reference token.core that way.",
      "parentUuid": "AAAAMX//3/A\u003d",
      "revId": "c0967193c679b2dacbc0b94245d43f7882d4e5b2",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
